sh, straight, or pair. My best five-card combination is Ace-High with kickers 9, 8, 7, 6 (using the 7 from my hand). This hand is quite weak, as it loses to any pair and any high card kicker T, J, Q, or K. However, I hold a 7, which acts as a blocker to the 5-7 straight and gives me a better kicker than opponents holding a 5, 3, or 2. The game history shows both players checking on the Pre-flop, Flop, and Turn. This passive line suggests the opponent also has a weak hand or a draw that missed. Since I am in the lead (+100 cumulative) and acting first on the River (or checking back), checking is the prudent play. A bet (bluff) is risky because my line of checking two streets does not represent strength, and I risk being called by a weak pair or King-high, which beats me. Checking costs nothing and allows me to win if the opponent holds a worse kicker.\n\nReason: My hand has minimal showdown value (Ace-high, 7 kicker), but the opponent has shown weakness by checking all streets. Bluffing is high-variance and risky here. Checking allows me to get to showdown for free, potentially winning against bottom-range hands (like 5-x or 2-x) while preserving my chip lead.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
