ying the board with a King kicker.\nThe opponent checked the Flop and Turn but made a massive overbet of 805 on the River into a small pot (approx 200 chips). This bet size (4x pot) is highly polarizing, representing either a very strong hand (Full House with a 2, 6, or 88, or a high pocket pair) or a bluff.\nCritically, your hand (King-high kicker) loses to **any Ace** (which makes Two Pair with Ace kicker), any pocket pair 77 or higher, and any 2 or 6. An Ace is a very common hand to reach the river with. Given the large bet size, the pot odds are poor (requiring ~44% equity), and you are likely behind the opponent's value range (Aces+) and only ahead of pure air. Preserving your chip lead and avoiding high-variance bluff-catching in this spot is the optimal strategy.\n\nReason: The opponent's large river overbet signifies strength or a polarized bluff. With only a King kicker playing the board, you lose to any Ace, any pocket pair 77+, and any 2 or 6. The probability of the opponent having an Ace or better is high enough that calling is negative expected value (-EV). Folding protects your stack and cumulative lead.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
